+**`SMAppService.daemon(plistName:).status == .notFound` — don't assume it's a bundle/signing bug.**
+DiskVerdict (2026-09-11/12) spent a full session chasing `.notFound` as if the daemon plist,
+`BundleProgram` path, or Team ID matching were wrong — all of which were already correct (plist
+sealed at `Contents/Library/LaunchDaemons/<label>.plist`, matching Team ID on app + helper, hardened
+runtime, notarized). Live instrumentation (temporary `.task` printing `service.status` /
+`register()`'s thrown error to stderr, run via `nohup <binary> &` — not `open`, so stderr is
+capturable — see cookbook pattern below) showed the *real* status was `.requiresApproval`, and
+`register()` was throwing the documented `SMAppServiceErrorDomain Code=1 "Operation not permitted"`
+for that state — a normal "needs a human click in System Settings → Login Items & Extensions" gate,
+not a validation failure. **`.notFound` can be the transient pre-registration status before BTM has
+ever recorded an attempt** (e.g., the very first `register()` call on a machine, or a process that
+quit before the XPC round-trip to `backgroundtaskmanagementd` finished persisting) — it is not
+reliably distinguishable from "bundle genuinely invalid" without actually calling `register()` and
+reading the thrown error, or re-checking `.status` on a subsequent launch. Don't diagnose from static
+files (plist/entitlements/codesign) alone when `.notFound` is reported — reproduce live first.
+
+**Reproducing `register()` output live, safely:** add a temporary `.task { print status before/after
+register() }` to the relevant SwiftUI view, force that view to render (e.g., default the app's tab/
+mode to it), build with the SAME signing identity as production (`DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=<team>
+CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application"` — ad-hoc signing has no stable Team ID and
+`SMAppService` will never validate it), run the built binary directly via `nohup <path> &` (a plain
+backgrounded `&` inside a tool call gets reaped when the tool call returns; `open` detaches cleanly
+but discards stdout/stderr since `FileHandle.standardError.write` bypasses os_log and won't show up
+in `log stream` either). Revert the instrumentation before committing.
+
